-- 1. sri-lanka-surf-tour
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka''s southwest coast serves up consistent swell from November to April, with breaks that work for everyone from absolute beginners to experienced surfers. Weligama is the main learning beach — long, gentle waves that roll for 50–80 metres, making it one of the easiest places on earth to stand up on a board for the first time.',
    intro = 'Sri Lanka''s southwest coast serves up consistent swell from November to April, with breaks that work for everyone from absolute beginners to experienced surfers. Weligama is the main learning beach — long, gentle waves that roll for 50–80 metres, making it one of the easiest places on earth to stand up on a board for the first time. A two-hour board rental costs about LKR 500, and a one-on-one lesson with an instructor runs LKR 2,500–3,500. Further along the coast, Midigama offers more challenging reef breaks like Lazy Left and Ramps, while Arugam Bay on the east coast takes over from May to October with world-class point breaks. You don''t need to bring your own board — nearly every beachfront shop rents decent foamies and fibreglass boards. The water stays warm year-round (27–30°C), so board shorts or a bikini and reef-safe sunscreen are all you really need.',
    where_to_experience = 'The main surf zones follow the monsoon patterns. On the southwest coast (Weligama, Midigama, Kabalana, Ahangama), the season runs November to April. Weligama Bay is the best place to learn — the wave breaks over a sandy bottom with no rocks or reef to worry about. Midigama, 10 minutes east, has reef breaks for intermediate and advanced surfers; don''t paddle out at Lazy Left without checking the reef first at low tide. On the east coast, Arugam Bay runs May to October, with the famous A-Frame and Whiskey Point breaks. Pottuvil Point, just north of Arugam Bay, is a long, peeling right-hander that can hold bigger swell. For a less crowded option, Peanut Farm at the northern end of Arugam Bay offers a gentler wave on smaller days. Rental shops cluster around Weligama town centre and the Arugam Bay main strip — most stores also offer storage overnight for about LKR 300.',
    tips_json = '["Rent a board for a few days before buying — Weligama''s rental quiver is solid and it saves hauling gear.","Check the tide chart: Weligama works best on a rising mid-tide, not dead low.","Reef booties are worth it at Midigama — the coral is sharp and sea urchins hide in the shallows.","Don''t leave valuables on the beach while you''re in the water — most surf shops have lockers for LKR 200.","November and March are the sweet spot: the swell is consistent but the beaches aren''t as packed as December–January."]',
    best_time = 'November to April on the southwest coast; May to October on the east coast around Arugam Bay. The shoulder months (March and October) offer the best balance of good waves and quiet beaches.',
    cta_text = 'Book your surf holiday'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-surf-tour'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 2. sigiriya-pidurangala-sunrise
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Climbing Pidurangala at dawn gives you the classic view of Sigiriya Rock rising from the mist — the shot you''ve seen on every travel feed, except you''re seeing it with your own eyes. The hike takes about 30 minutes up a rocky trail with a few scrambling sections near the top.',
    intro = 'Climbing Pidurangala at dawn gives you the classic view of Sigiriya Rock rising from the mist — the shot you''ve seen on every travel feed, except you''re seeing it with your own eyes. The hike takes about 30 minutes up a rocky trail with a few scrambling sections near the top, nothing technical but you''ll want proper shoes, not flip-flops. Entry to Pidurangala costs LKR 1,000, a fraction of Sigiriya''s $30 fee, and the view from the summit platform is arguably better because you''re looking at the rock itself, not standing on it. After coming down, head over to Sigiriya to explore the water gardens, frescos, and mirror wall — the site opens at 7am and you''ll beat the tour buses if you get there before 9am. The two sites together make a perfect half-day combo.',
    where_to_experience = 'Pidurangala Rock is about 2 km north of Sigiriya Rock, a quick tuk-tuk ride from most hotels in the area. The trail starts behind the Pidurangala Raja Maha Vihara temple — park at the temple lot and follow the path. The first half is an easy dirt track through boulders, then it gets steeper with some rock-scrambling near the top. There are a couple of spots where you''ll need to use your hands, so leave the big camera bag behind. Sigiriya itself is 20 minutes south on the main road, well signposted. Most guesthouses in Sigiriya town can arrange a tuk-tuk to drop you at Pidurangala for the sunrise and pick you up after you come down. The Sigiriya museum at the base of the rock is worth 30 minutes before you leave the area.',
    tips_json = '["Start the climb by 5:15am to be on top for sunrise — the gate opens at 5am sharp.","Wear trainers or sturdy sandals, not flip-flops. The last 50 metres involve actual bouldering.","Bring a small torch or use your phone light — the trail is completely dark before dawn.","Buy water at the temple shop before you start; there''s nothing on the trail.","The mist clears fastest between 6:30 and 7:30am — that''s your window for the classic photo."]',
    best_time = 'December to May is the dry season in the Cultural Triangle. The early morning mist is most dramatic from January to March. Avoid October–November when the trail gets slippery from rain.',
    cta_text = 'Book the sunrise climb'
WHERE slug = 'sigiriya-pidurangala-sunrise'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 3. ancient-culture-and-temples
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka''s Cultural Triangle packs more ancient history into a 70 km radius than most countries have in total. The three main sites — Anuradhapura, Polonnaruwa, and Kandy — span over 2,000 years of civilisation, with dagobas the size of small hills, rock-cut Buddha statues, and temple complexes that still function as living religious centres.',
    intro = 'Sri Lanka''s Cultural Triangle packs more ancient history into a 70 km radius than most countries have in total. The three main sites — Anuradhapura, Polonnaruwa, and Kandy — span over 2,000 years of civilisation, with dagobas the size of small hills, rock-cut Buddha statues, and temple complexes that still function as living religious centres. Anuradhapura was the first capital, founded in the 4th century BCE, and its sacred Bo Tree is the oldest historically documented tree in the world. Polonnaruwa, the medieval capital, has the best-preserved ruins including the Gal Vihara rock-cut Buddhas. Kandy''s Temple of the Tooth holds a relic that draws pilgrims from across the Buddhist world. A combined ticket for Anuradhapura and Polonnaruwa costs $25 and is valid for two weeks — keep your ticket safe.',
    where_to_experience = 'Start in Anuradhapura (3 hours north of Colombo by train). The main sites: Sri Maha Bodhi (the sacred Bo Tree), Ruwanwelisaya dagoba, Jetavanaramaya (once the tallest brick structure in the world), and the Abhayagiri monastery complex. Hire a bicycle or tuk-tuk for the day — the site is massive and walking isn''t practical. Polonnaruwa is 2 hours east; the Royal Palace complex, the Quadrangle, and Gal Vihara are the highlights. Hire a bicycle here too — the ruins stretch along a 3 km road. Kandy is 2 hours south of Polonnaruwa through beautiful hill country. The Temple of the Tooth is in the city centre; visit in the morning (5:30–8am) or during the evening puja at 6:30pm to see the ceremonies. Between these three, build in stops at Mihintale (where Buddhism arrived in Sri Lanka), Dambulla Cave Temple, and the Aukana Buddha, a 12-metre rock-cut statue.',
    tips_json = '["Get the Cultural Triangle combined ticket ($25) — it covers Anuradhapura and Polonnaruwa and saves you about $10.","Start each site at 7am before the heat and crowds arrive. By 11am the stone floors are too hot for bare feet.","You''ll need to remove shoes and cover shoulders/knees at every temple — bring a sarong; it works for all of them.","Hire a guide at the entrance of each site for about LKR 1,500–2,000 — the stories behind the ruins make the visit.","The Tropic Inn in Anuradhapura does a great rice and curry lunch for LKR 600."]',
    best_time = 'January to April is the ideal window — dry, sunny, and not too hot. May to August brings the southwest monsoon rains. The Cultural Triangle is busy during the August Esala Perahera festival in Kandy.',
    cta_text = 'Explore Sri Lanka''s ancient cities'
WHERE slug = 'ancient-culture-and-temples'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 4. kandy-to-ella-train
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'The train ride from Kandy to Ella is regularly called one of the most beautiful train journeys in the world, and for once the hype is accurate. The 7-hour, 200 km route climbs from Kandy''s hill-country valleys through tea plantations, past waterfalls, and into the misty highlands around Nuwara Eliya before descending to Ella.',
    intro = 'The train ride from Kandy to Ella is regularly called one of the most beautiful train journeys in the world, and for once the hype is accurate. The 7-hour, 200 km route climbs from Kandy''s hill-country valleys through tea plantations, past waterfalls, and into the misty highlands around Nuwara Eliya before descending to Ella. The most scenic section is between Hatton and Haputale, where the train crawls through cloud forest, crosses viaducts, and gives you open views across valleys carpeted in tea. You can book first-class observation car (LKR 2,100), second-class reserved (LKR 950), or third-class unreserved (LKR 380). The observation car has big windows and air conditioning, but you''ll get better photos hanging out of second-class carriage doors. Book at least 2–3 weeks ahead for reserved seats — unreserved sells out on the day.',
    where_to_experience = 'The train departs from Kandy railway station, about 1 km from the Temple of the Tooth. Boarding starts 30 minutes before departure, and it''s worth being early to grab a window seat on the right-hand side (the best views are on the right heading to Ella). The station has a small canteen with tea and snacks. Most people ride the full route Kandy–Ella, but you can also join at Hatton or Nanu Oya (the station for Nuwara Eliya) for a shorter ride through the best scenery. The train stops at every station, and vendors hop on at Hatton and Haputale selling freshly roasted cashews, mango slices, and spicy vadai. The Ella station is a 10-minute walk from the town centre. If you''re heading to Arugam Bay or Yala after, the train continues to Badulla where you can catch a bus south.',
    tips_json = '["Book second-class reserved (LKR 950) at least 2 weeks ahead on the Sri Lanka Railways website — the observation car is overrated.","The right-hand side of the train has the best views heading from Kandy to Ella.","Bring your own snacks and water; the station stalls are limited and the onboard vendor can run out.","Sit with your bag on your lap in unreserved carriages — pickpocketing happens in the crush near the doors.","If you can''t get a seat for the whole route, get on at Hatton (2.5 hours in) — the best scenery starts there anyway."]',
    best_time = 'Best from January to April when the hill country is driest. December and May still work but expect passing showers. Avoid the monsoon months of June–August and October–November when landslides can cancel trains on short notice.',
    cta_text = 'Book the train journey'
WHERE slug = 'kandy-to-ella-train'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 5. scenic-train-journeys
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Beyond the famous Kandy–Ella line, Sri Lanka has several other rail routes that cut through tea country, hug the coast, and cross spectacular viaducts. The colonial-built network, now a bit creaky, still offers some of the most dramatic train travel you can do anywhere in Asia for under $5.',
    intro = 'Beyond the famous Kandy–Ella line, Sri Lanka has several other rail routes that cut through tea country, hug the coast, and cross spectacular viaducts. The colonial-built network, now a bit creaky, still offers some of the most dramatic train travel you can do anywhere in Asia for under $5. The Colombo–Badulla overnight train runs the hill-country line in the dark but the sunrise over the tea estates as you approach Haputale is a genuine travel thrill. The coastal line from Colombo to Galle and Matara runs right along the beach south of Kalutara — turquoise water on one side, palm trees on the other, for about LKR 180. The Northern Line to Jaffna, reopened in 2015 after decades of war, crosses massive dry-zone plains and giant lagoons. For pure spectacle, the Demodara Loop near Ella is a spiral section where the train loops around itself inside a tunnel.',
    where_to_experience = 'The Main Line runs Colombo to Badulla via Kandy, Nanu Oya, and Ella — the route that passes through 44 tunnels and over dozens of bridges, including the famous Nine Arch Bridge near Ella. The Coastal Line runs Colombo to Matara via Galle, and the most scenic stretch is between Colombo and Aluthgama where the tracks run within metres of the beach. The Northern Line runs Colombo to Jaffna via Anuradhapura and Vavuniya — the section across the Elephant Pass lagoon is stunning at sunset. The Kelani Valley Line runs from Colombo to Avissawella through rubber and tea plantations, a slow but beautiful ride few tourists take. All routes are operated by Sri Lanka Railways with diesel locomotives on most services. Timetables change seasonally — check at the station the day before.',
    tips_json = '["The Colombo–Galle coastal train costs LKR 180 and runs every hour — get a seat on the left side for ocean views.","Demodara Loop is best photographed from the hillside above the tunnel entrance; walk up from the Demodara station stop.","The overnight Colombo–Badulla train (7pm–7am) is cold — bring a jacket even in summer.","First-class AC on the coastal line has sealed windows that block the sea breeze; second-class is better.","Download the train schedule PDF from Sri Lanka Railways before you arrive — their website isn''t mobile-friendly."]',
    best_time = 'January to April for hill-country routes (Main Line). November to April for the coastal line. The Northern Line is rideable year-round but December–February is cooler for the dry zone crossing.',
    cta_text = 'Ride Sri Lanka''s scenic railways'
WHERE slug = 'scenic-train-journeys'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 6. yala-leopard-safari
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Yala National Park has one of the highest densities of leopards anywhere in the world — roughly one leopard per square kilometre in Block 1. A good safari here means watching these cats laze on granite boulders or pad along scrubland tracks, often within 20 metres of your jeep.',
    intro = 'Yala National Park has one of the highest densities of leopards anywhere in the world — roughly one leopard per square kilometre in Block 1. A good safari here means watching these cats laze on granite boulders or pad along scrubland tracks, often within 20 metres of your jeep. The park covers 979 sq km divided into five blocks. Block 1, the most popular and the only one open year-round, has the best leopard sightings. A half-day safari (5am–10am or 2pm–6pm) costs about LKR 5,000–8,000 per person including jeep, driver, and guide, plus the park entrance fee (LKR 3,000 for adults). The jeeps are open-sided Toyota Hiluxes — bumpy, dusty, but perfectly suited for the terrain. Morning safaris are better for cats; afternoon safaris are better for elephants and sloth bears.',
    where_to_experience = 'Yala''s Block 1 entrance is at Palatupana, about 25 km east of Tissamaharama. Most visitors stay in Tissa (cheap guesthouses, lots of restaurants) or in the Cinnamon Wild resort just outside the park gate. The safari route within the park is fixed by the drivers — they radio each other when they spot something. The main leopard hangouts are the rocky outcrops near Sithulpawwa and around the Mahaseelawa tank. Elephants are common around the Patanangala beach area. Birdlife is incredible — you''ll see painted storks, hornbills, eagles, and peacocks even on a slow day. The park also has a surprising number of crocodiles, muggers and saltwater both, lounging around the waterholes. Book your safari through your hotel or one of the many tour desks on Tissa''s main road — prices are negotiable outside peak season.',
    tips_json = '["Book a full-day safari (LKR 10,000–12,000) if you can — the light is better and you''ll have the park almost to yourself after 10am when the half-day groups leave.","The south entrance (Bundala side) is less crowded than Palatupana; ask your driver to enter there.","Wear neutral colours — bright whites and reds stand out to the animals and your driver will curse you.","Bring binoculars, not just a phone camera. Half the sightings are in the trees 50 metres off the track.","Don''t expect a leopard every time; Yala delivers about a 70–80% sighting rate. The elephants, crocodiles, and birdlife make it worth it anyway."]',
    best_time = 'February to October is prime season. The park closes for the dry season maintenance in September annually (dates vary — check ahead). November–January has fewer visitors but leopards are harder to spot in the tall grass after the rains.',
    cta_text = 'Book your Yala safari'
WHERE slug = 'yala-leopard-safari'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 7. tea-country-and-plantations
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Up in Sri Lanka''s central highlands, the tea country stretches across rolling green hills at altitudes above 1,500 metres. This is where your morning cup of Ceylon tea comes from — and visiting a working plantation shows you just how much work goes into those little leaves.',
    intro = 'Up in Sri Lanka''s central highlands, the tea country stretches across rolling green hills at altitudes above 1,500 metres. This is where your morning cup of Ceylon tea comes from — and visiting a working plantation shows you just how much work goes into those little leaves. The best-known region is Nuwara Eliya, which produces bright, light teas with a golden colour. Further south, Haputale and Ella produce darker, stronger brews. A standard plantation tour costs LKR 1,500 and includes walking through the fields, watching the withering and rolling process in the factory, and tasting several grades. The Damro Labookellie Tea Centre (formerly Mackwoods) near Nuwara Eliya is the most visitor-friendly, with a free tour and a great cake-and-tea combo for LKR 400. Most plantations employ Tamil women pluckers who live on the estate — they''ll usually smile and wave if you say good morning as they work.',
    where_to_experience = 'Nuwara Eliya is the centre of Sri Lanka''s tea country. The town itself is a chilly colonial hill station — think red-brick post offices, golf courses, and the odd English gentleman. The tea factories are scattered along the A5 highway between Nuwara Eliya and Haputale. Blue Field Tea Factory (about 10 km from Nuwara Eliya) has a good tour for LKR 1,500 with tasting included. Pedro Tea Estate is closer to town and easier to reach if you''re short on time. Further south, the Haputale area has the Lipton''s Seat viewpoint, where Sir Thomas Lipton supposedly sat to survey his empire — the 360-degree view over tea-covered hills is worth the bumpy road up. If you''re in Ella, Halpewatte Tea Factory offers tours with a small museum and shop. The Uva region around Haputale produces the best black teas for milk tea drinkers.',
    tips_json = '["Visit a working factory before noon — the tea processing (withering, rolling, fermenting) happens in the morning and the factory is quiet after lunch.","Don''t buy your tea at the tourist shops in town; buy directly from the factory shop for better prices and fresher stock.","The drive between Nuwara Eliya and Haputale on the A5 takes 2 hours for 80 km — the winding road is slow but the views are the best in the country.","Bring a fleece or jacket; Nuwara Eliya sits at 1,868 metres and the temperature drops to 10°C at night even in summer.","The pluckers work 6am–3pm for about LKR 800 a day — if you photograph them, offer a small tip or buy tea from the estate shop."]',
    best_time = 'January to March for the clearest mountain views. April–May and September–November are the harvesting seasons when the fields are most vividly green. June–August is rainy and misty — you''ll miss the views.',
    cta_text = 'Tour the tea plantations'
WHERE slug = 'tea-country-and-plantations'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 8. udawalawe-elephant-safari
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Udawalawe is Sri Lanka''s best bet for wild elephants, full stop. The park''s open grasslands and reservoir attract herds of 30–60 elephants at a time, often visible from the main road before you even pay the entrance fee. You''ll see elephants here — not maybe, not probably.',
    intro = 'Udawalawe is Sri Lanka''s best bet for wild elephants, full stop. The park''s open grasslands and reservoir attract herds of 30–60 elephants at a time, often visible from the main road before you even pay the entrance fee. You''ll see elephants here — not maybe, not probably. The park covers 308 sq km of open savannah with scattered forests, and the lack of dense cover means you can see animals from a long way off. A standard 3-hour safari costs about LKR 4,000–5,000 per person including jeep and driver, plus the park entrance fee of LKR 2,500. Morning safaris from 6am to 9am catch the elephants at their most active as they come down to the reservoir to drink. The afternoon safari (3pm–6pm) is hotter but the light is better for photos. The park is compact enough that you''ll cover most of it in a half-day trip.',
    where_to_experience = 'The park entrance is on the A18 highway, about 15 km from the town of Udawalawe and 2 hours from Ella on the main road south. Most visitors coming from the hill country do Udawalawe as a stopover en route to the south coast. The safari route follows the park''s network of dirt roads, looping around the reservoir and across the plains. The elephants tend to gather around the Walawe River and the reservoir edge in the dry season. There''s also a surprisingly large population of water buffalo, crocodiles, and deer. The Elephant Transit Home, just outside the park, rehabilitates orphaned baby elephants and releases them back into the wild — visiting at feeding time (9am, 12pm, 3pm, 6pm) is a good addition to the safari. The viewing platform there overlooks the feeding enclosure and costs LKR 500.',
    tips_json = '["Book a jeep through your hotel for about LKR 4,500 total (not per person); the safari operators near the gate overcharge walk-ins.","Bring a hat and sunscreen — the grasslands have almost no shade and the sun is ferocious by 9am.","If you''re driving yourself, the park roads are rutted dirt — a regular car won''t make it; hire a jeep.","The Elephant Transit Home feeding sessions at 9am and 3pm are the least crowded.","Combine Udawalawe with a transfer: most drivers from Ella will detour through the park and drop you at the south coast for no extra cost."]',
    best_time = 'The park is open year-round. The best visibility is in the dry season (May–September) when elephants congregate around the shrinking waterholes. December–February brings more greenery and fewer vehicles but the elephants are harder to spot.',
    cta_text = 'Book the elephant safari'
WHERE slug = 'udawalawe-elephant-safari'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 9. minneriya-elephant-gathering
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Minneriya National Park hosts one of Asia''s most dramatic wildlife spectacles: the Gathering, where hundreds of wild elephants converge around the Minneriya Tank during the dry season. Numbers peak at 300–400 elephants in a single view, gathered on the grassy edges of the reservoir.',
    intro = 'Minneriya National Park hosts one of Asia''s most dramatic wildlife spectacles: the Gathering, where hundreds of wild elephants converge around the Minneriya Tank during the dry season. Numbers peak at 300–400 elephants in a single view, gathered on the grassy edges of the reservoir. The phenomenon happens because the surrounding jungle dries up between July and October, leaving the Minneriya reservoir as the only reliable water and fresh grass source for miles. The elephants come down in family groups from the surrounding forests and spend the day feeding, bathing, and socialising. A safari costs LKR 4,500–5,500 per person including jeep and driver, plus the park entrance fee (LKR 2,500 for adults). The viewing is best in the late afternoon (3pm–6pm) when the elephants are most active and the light turns golden.',
    where_to_experience = 'Minneriya is in the North Central Province, about 20 km from Habarana — the main tourist hub for the area. The park entrance is on the Habarana–Trincomalee road. The Gathering is viewable from the main road in the late afternoon, but a jeep safari lets you drive along the reservoir edge and get closer (but not too close — 30 metres is the minimum distance). The adjacent Kaudulla National Park, 10 km north, has a similar gathering that peaks later in the season (September–December) and is less crowded. Many drivers will take you to both parks in a single afternoon if the elephants aren''t showing at Minneriya. The landscape is flat, dry, and dotted with giant termite mounds — not as pretty as Yala or Udawalawe, but the sheer number of elephants makes up for it. Habarana has dozens of guesthouses and hotels, from basic rooms (LKR 3,000) to luxury resorts.',
    tips_json = '["Go between July and October for the peak Gathering numbers; November still works but the herds are smaller.","An afternoon safari (2:30pm onwards) is better than morning — the elephants arrive at the reservoir around 3pm.","Binoculars are essential here; you''ll want to watch herd interactions from a respectful distance.","Ask your driver to check Kaudulla too — if Minneriya is quiet, Kaudulla often has the herds instead.","Pack insect repellent; the reservoir edge gets swarms of mosquitoes at dusk."]',
    best_time = 'July to October is the peak Gathering season. The largest numbers are usually in August and September. November still has good sightings but the elephants start dispersing as the rains fill the other water sources.',
    cta_text = 'Book the Gathering safari'
WHERE slug = 'minneriya-elephant-gathering'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 10. beaches-and-coastal-life-sri-lanka
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka''s coastline runs for 1,600 km and comes in three distinct varieties: the southwest''s golden tourist beaches, the south''s rugged coves and surf breaks, and the east coast''s palm-fringed stretches of white sand. Each has a different vibe, different season, and different reasons to stay.',
    intro = 'Sri Lanka''s coastline runs for 1,600 km and comes in three distinct varieties: the southwest''s golden tourist beaches, the south''s rugged coves and surf breaks, and the east coast''s palm-fringed stretches of white sand. Each has a different vibe, different season, and different reasons to stay. The southwest coast — Negombo, Bentota, Hikkaduwa, and down to Galle — is the most developed, with beach bars, water sports, and resorts at every turn. The south coast from Galle to Tangalle has smaller, quieter beaches like Dalawella, Koggala, and Hiriketiya, where you can rent a room in a family-run guesthouse for LKR 3,000 a night. The east coast — Nilaveli, Trincomalee, Pasikuda, and Arugam Bay — has the whitest sand and clearest water but only works from May to October. Between November and April, the east coast is hit by the northeast monsoon and most places shut down.',
    where_to_experience = 'Southwest beaches (November–April): Hikkaduwa is the party beach with coral reefs for snorkelling right off the sand. Bentota is calmer, good for families and jet-ski rides. Unawatuna is a horseshoe bay with restaurants built into the hillside — it''s busy but the sunset is worth it. South coast beaches (November–April): Hiriketiya near Dikwella is a perfect half-moon bay with a few cafes and surfers. Talalla is quieter, with crystal-clear water and a small yoga community. Tangalle has multiple coves — Goyambokka and Marakolliya are the best for swimming. East coast beaches (May–October): Nilaveli has 4 km of white sand and the Pigeon Island snorkelling trip for LKR 3,000. Pasikuda is waist-deep for hundreds of metres — the safest swimming beach in Sri Lanka. Arugam Bay is the main surf town, but the beach itself is fine sand with good swimming.',
    tips_json = '["Check the season: southwest beaches work November–April, east coast works May–October. Show up in the wrong season and you''ll have rough seas and closed restaurants.","Hiriketiya is the best all-rounder beach in the south — good swimming, good food, good vibe, without the Hikkaduwa chaos.","Nilaveli beach has the strongest currents on the east coast — swim between the flags at the main hotel stretch.","Try a beach that doesn''t appear in the guidebooks: Piniwana (near Tangalle) and Siyambalagoda (near Matara) are locals-only and almost empty.","Beach chairs at the main tourist beaches cost LKR 500 for the day — negotiate. A beer on the beach runs LKR 400–600 at most places."]',
    best_time = 'November to April for the southwest and south coasts. May to October for the east coast. The shoulder months (April and October) let you catch two coasts in good condition if you''re moving around the island.',
    cta_text = 'Find your perfect beach'
WHERE slug = 'beaches-and-coastal-life-sri-lanka'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 11. galle-fort-southern-coast
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Galle Fort is the best-preserved colonial fortification in Asia, built by the Dutch in the 17th century on a promontory that Portuguese sailors had fortified a century earlier. Walking the rampart walls at sunset, with the Indian Ocean on one side and the old town''s tiled roofs on the other, is the single best free thing you can do in Sri Lanka.',
    intro = 'Galle Fort is the best-preserved colonial fortification in Asia, built by the Dutch in the 17th century on a promontory that Portuguese sailors had fortified a century earlier. Walking the rampart walls at sunset, with the Indian Ocean on one side and the old town''s tiled roofs on the other, is the single best free thing you can do in Sri Lanka. The fort walls run for about 3 km around the old town, dotted with bastions named after Dutch cities — Utrecht, Amsterdam, Rotterdam. Inside the walls, narrow streets are lined with Dutch-era buildings housing boutique hotels, cinnamon-scented cafes, gem shops, and art galleries. The National Museum in the old Dutch building (LKR 500 entry) displays maritime artefacts from the harbour''s trading heyday. The fort is both a UNESCO World Heritage site and a living neighbourhood — people still live, work, and go to school inside the walls. The Galle Lighthouse at the fort''s southern tip marks the start of the best walking route around the ramparts.',
    where_to_experience = 'The fort sits on a peninsula at the southwest corner of Galle town, about 2 hours south of Colombo by train or road. Enter through the Main Gate on the north side, where the clock tower marks the pedestrian entrance. The best walking route: head south along the eastern ramparts past the Maritime Museum, turn right at the flag rock (where the Dutch flag pole still stands), and follow the western wall back past the cricket ground. Allow 45 minutes for a relaxed loop with photo stops. The old town inside the fort is walkable and compact — everything is within a 15-minute walk. Key stops: the Old Dutch Church (1640), the Meera Mosque with its Dutch-colonial architecture, the National Museum, and the lighthouse. Dozens of cafes line Church Street and Pedlar Street — try the tuna sandwich at The Shack or the buffalo curd with treacle at the Fort Health Restaurant. The Galle Fort Hotel does a good high tea (LKR 1,800) if you want the colonial experience.',
    tips_json = '["Walk the ramparts at 5pm — the heat drops, the light turns golden, and the cricket ground below comes alive with evening matches.","The fort is free to enter and wander; only the museum and private buildings charge admission.","Skip the tour buses that cram the main streets between 10am and 2pm; the fort empties out beautifully in the late afternoon.","Church Street on a Sunday morning is the quietest time to visit — most shops stay closed and you get the streets to yourself.","You can hire a guide for about LKR 1,500 for a 2-hour walk; worth it if you want the history of each bastion."]',
    best_time = 'January to April for dry, sunny weather on the south coast. November and December have some rain but the fort is quieter. Avoid the main gate area between 10am–2pm when cruise ship day-trippers pour in.',
    cta_text = 'Walk the ramparts of Galle Fort'
WHERE slug = 'galle-fort-southern-coast'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 12. mirissa-whale-watching
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Mirissa is the whale-watching capital of Sri Lanka, sitting at the edge of the deep submarine canyon that funnels blue whales, sperm whales, and pods of spinner dolphins right past the coast. Between November and April, sightings of blue whales run at 90%+ — about as close to a guarantee as wildlife-watching gets.',
    intro = 'Mirissa is the whale-watching capital of Sri Lanka, sitting at the edge of the deep submarine canyon that funnels blue whales, sperm whales, and pods of spinner dolphins right past the coast. Between November and April, sightings of blue whales run at 90%+ — about as close to a guarantee as wildlife-watching gets. A standard tour costs LKR 6,000–8,000 per person for a shared boat, including breakfast, lunch, and hotel pickup. The boats leave at 6am sharp — earlier if you want to beat the crowds — and the trip runs about 4–5 hours. You''ll head about 15–25 km offshore to where the continental shelf drops off. Besides blue whales (the largest animals ever to have lived on earth), you''ll commonly see Bryde''s whales, sperm whales, and massive pods of spinner dolphins that perform aerial tricks alongside the boat. The operators are regulated and required to maintain distance, but some boats still crowd individual whales. Go with a reputable operator that belongs to the Sri Lanka Whale Watching Association.',
    where_to_experience = 'The whale-watching boats depart from Mirissa harbour, a 10-minute walk from the main beach. The harbour is small and chaotic — your operator will tell you which boat to find. Most operators run two trips daily: the 6am morning tour (best, calmest seas) and a 10am tour (windier, more chop, more likely to miss the whales). The whale-watching season extends from Mirissa south and east along the coast — you can also go from Weligama, Galle, and even Tangalle, but Mirissa has the most boats and the most experienced crews. The deep ocean canyon where the whales feed is about 20 km south of Mirissa, and the journey out takes about 1 hour. The boats slow down once they reach the drop-off and the spotter starts looking for blows. The return journey can be rough if the wind picks up — if you''re prone to seasickness, this is the part that gets you.',
    tips_json = '["Take seasickness medicine the night before and again in the morning — ginger tablets work but Dramamine is stronger; the boat is fine on the way out but the stationary drifting can turn your stomach.","Book a smaller boat (max 20 people) for a better experience; the 60-person party boats are loud and crowded.","The front of the boat has the smoothest ride and the best view; sit there unless you want a soaking from spray.","Early April has the best conditions: calm seas, good sightings, and fewer boats than in January–February peak.","Don''t bring a drone — the operators won''t let you fly it and the crew will confiscate it until you dock."]',
    best_time = 'November to April is the main season. Peak whale activity and calmest seas are January to March. December can have rough seas. Avoid May to October when the southwest monsoon makes the ocean too dangerous for tours.',
    cta_text = 'Book a whale-watching tour'
WHERE slug = 'mirissa-whale-watching'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 13. soft-adventure
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Soft adventure in Sri Lanka means things like kayaking through mangroves in Bentota, cycling through paddy fields in the hill country, paddleboarding at dawn on a quiet river, and hiking up little-known peaks that don''t require ropes or guides. It''s the middle ground between lounging on a sunbed and a full expedition.',
    intro = 'Soft adventure in Sri Lanka means things like kayaking through mangroves in Bentota, cycling through paddy fields in the hill country, paddleboarding at dawn on a quiet river, and hiking up little-known peaks that don''t require ropes or guides. It''s the middle ground between lounging on a sunbed and a full expedition. The Madu Ganga river near Balapitiya is a network of mangrove islands you can explore by kayak for LKR 1,500 for a two-hour guided trip — the guides show you monitor lizards, water monitors, and the floating cinnamon gardens. Cycling tours in the Waddington area near Kandy take you through tea smallholdings and villages where people still grind spices by hand. Stand-up paddleboarding (SUP) on the calm backwaters of the Bentota River at sunrise costs LKR 2,000 with a board and basic instruction. Several operators run gentle two-hour hikes to Little Adam''s Peak in Ella (no relation to Adam''s Peak — this one takes 45 minutes and is a wide dirt path) and the Uva Halpewatta Loop through tea plantations.',
    where_to_experience = 'Bentota River and Madu Ganga (southwest coast) are the best spots for kayaking and paddleboarding — the water is flat, the mangroves are dense with wildlife, and the current is negligible. The Bentota River Tourism Zone has several operators. For cycling, the back roads around Kandy, the Gampola valley, and the Tissamaharama lake area are flat or gently rolling, with minimal traffic outside the main roads. The road around the Tissa Wewa lake is a perfect 6 km loop. For hiking, the hill country is full of short trails that don''t require a guide. In Ella, Little Adam''s Peak and the Bambarakanda Falls trail (Sri Lanka''s tallest waterfall, 263 metres) are both accessible to anyone with basic fitness. In the central highlands, hikes around Horton Plains (World''s End, Baker''s Falls) are on flat, well-maintained trails and don''t involve climbing. Horton Plains entry is LKR 2,500 for adults and the full loop is about 10 km.',
    tips_json = '["The Bentota River SUP trips are best at 6:30am before the speedboats start churning up the water.","For cycling, bring your own padded shorts — the rental bikes in Kandy have the original rock-hard saddles.","Horton Plains is cold (5–10°C) at dawn — bring a windproof jacket even if it feels warm in Nuwara Eliya.","Bambarakanda Falls is best just after the rains (April and November) when the water flow is heavy.","Most soft-adventure operators don''t require advance booking — just show up before 8am and negotiate."]',
    best_time = 'November to April for kayaking and SUP on the southwest coast (flat water, no monsoon chop). January to March for hiking in the hill country (clear trails, no rain). Avoid July–August for river activities when the currents are stronger.',
    cta_text = 'Find your soft adventure'
WHERE slug = 'soft-adventure'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 14. tea-country-nuwara-eliya
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Nuwara Eliya sits at 1,868 metres in the central highlands. It''s a cool-climate hill station where you can stay in a colonial-era bungalow with a fireplace, eat a full English breakfast at a club that still observes dress codes, and walk through tea plantations that produce some of the best Ceylon tea on earth.',
    intro = 'Nuwara Eliya sits at 1,868 metres in the central highlands. It''s a cool-climate hill station where you can stay in a colonial-era bungalow with a fireplace, eat a full English breakfast at a club that still observes dress codes, and walk through tea plantations that produce some of the best Ceylon tea on earth. The town centres around the hill pond (Lake Gregory), a Victorian-era post office built from red brick, and the Grand Hotel (formerly the governor''s residence). The surrounding hills are covered in tea plantations that produce the bright, golden liquors characteristic of Nuwara Eliya distr The Pedro tea estate, 3 km from town, offers a free walk-through of the factory and a tasting room. The town itself is busy with tuk-tuks and day-trippers from Colombo, but step 500 metres off the main street and you''re walking through quiet lanes lined with eucalyptus and cypress trees. Night temperatures drop to 5–10°C — pack accordingly.',
    where_to_experience = 'Nuwara Eliya is 180 km from Colombo (about 5 hours by winding hill road, or you can take the train to Nanu Oya station and then a 20-minute tuk-tuk). The town centre is compact. Key spots: Lake Gregory (LKR 200 entry for a lakeside walk, pony rides, and paddle boats), Victoria Park (well-maintained flower gardens, LKR 500), and the golf course (one of Asia''s oldest, 18 holes, green fee LKR 4,000). Nearby plantations: Pedro Tea Estate (free entry, factory tour available), Blue Field Tea Gardens (their vanilla tea is worth buying), and Damro Labookellie (the Mackwoods sign is gone but the tour is the same). The walk from Nuwara Eliya to the Lovers Leap waterfall is 2 km each way and takes you through tea fields with open valley views. For the classic hill-club experience, afternoon tea at the Grand Hotel (LKR 1,500) is worth the indulgence. The Nuwara Eliya market on New Bazaar Street sells fresh strawberries, carrots, and leeks that grow in the valley — the best produce in Sri Lanka.',
    tips_json = '["Bring several layers; Nuwara Eliya gets colder than you expect — 10°C at night is normal and some guesthouses have no heating.","The Pedro Tea Estate walk is free and the quality of the tea shop is better than the tourist-oriented Blue Field.","Lake Gregory is best in the late afternoon when the light hits the hills; avoid it on weekends when it''s packed with Colombo day-trippers.","Eat at the local rice-and-curry joints on New Bazaar Street — the tourist restaurants on the main strip charge double for worse food.","The 5-hour drive from Colombo is winding and slow; take the train to Nanu Oya instead — the journey is part of the experience."]',
    best_time = 'January to March for the clearest skies and best views of the surrounding hills. April has the blooming rhododendrons and the Nuwara Eliya Horse Races. May–August is rainy and misty — you''ll see fog more than the landscape.',
    cta_text = 'Explore Nuwara Eliya'
WHERE slug = 'tea-country-nuwara-eliya'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 15. ayurveda-and-wellness
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Ayurveda in Sri Lanka isn''t a spa treatment you add on to a holiday — it''s a full medical system that''s been practised here for over 3,000 years. A proper ayurvedic experience means a consultation with a doctor, a personalised regimen of herbal treatments, oil massages, steam baths, and a strict diet designed to balance your dosha.',
    intro = 'Ayurveda in Sri Lanka isn''t a spa treatment you add on to a holiday — it''s a full medical system that''s been practised here for over 3,000 years. A proper ayurvedic experience means a consultation with a doctor, a personalised regimen of herbal treatments, oil massages, steam baths, and a strict diet designed to balance your dosha. The island has hundreds of Ayurveda centres, from basic roadside clinics to luxury retreats. A standard 3-day wellness package costs between LKR 25,000 and LKR 60,000 depending on the centre and whether accommodation is included. A single Ayurveda massage session runs LKR 3,000–5,000, and a consultation with a qualified doctor is around LKR 2,000. The Sri Lankan tradition of Ayurveda is slightly different from the Indian one — it places more emphasis on herbal decoctions and dietary therapy than on vigorous massage. Most treatments begin with a pulse diagnosis where the doctor reads your dosha through your wrist pulse.',
    where_to_experience = 'The south and southwest coasts have the highest concentration of Ayurveda centres. The area between Bentota and Beruwala is known as the Ayurveda Coast — dozens of dedicated resorts line the beach. The Siddhalepa Ayurveda Health Resort in Wadduwa is one of the oldest and most respected, with doctors who have been practising for 30+ years. The Barberyn chain (Beruwala and Weligama) combines traditional treatment with modern facilities. For something more affordable, the Lanka Herbals Ayur-Aroma in Galle offers individual treatments without the resort markup. In Kandy, the Suwisetha Ayurveda centre near the lake does good consultations. In the hill country, the surroundings of Nuwara Eliya are ideal for Panchakarma cleansing treatments — the cool climate helps the body absorb the oils. Most centres require a minimum 3-day stay for any meaningful treatment programme; a single massage is pleasant but doesn''t give you the full ayurvedic benefit.',
    tips_json = '["Do a pulse diagnosis consultation first (LKR 2,000) before booking treatments — the doctor will tell you what your body actually needs, not just sell you the most expensive package.","Ayurveda diet is part of the treatment; expect bland, salt-free, oil-free food for the first couple of days while your system cleanses.","Book at least 3 days minimum for any real benefit; a single treatment is nice but won''t balance anything.","The morning herbal steam (Swedana) is the most intense part — drink plenty of water afterwards.","Tell the doctor about any medications you''re on; some Ayurvedic herbs interact with blood pressure and diabetes medications."]',
    best_time = 'November to April is the traditional Ayurveda season in Sri Lanka. The dry, warm weather on the south coast allows the body to respond better to the oil treatments. Avoid the rainy season (May–August) when the humidity can make treatments less effective.',
    cta_text = 'Begin your Ayurveda journey'
WHERE slug = 'ayurveda-and-wellness'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 16. south-coast-surf-lessons
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Weligama is the best place on earth to learn to surf. The wave breaks over sand, rolls gently for 80 metres, and peels left and right so you don''t have to worry about which direction to go. It''s surf-training-wheels, and it works.',
    intro = 'Weligama is the best place on earth to learn to surf. The wave breaks over sand, rolls gently for 80 metres, and peels left and right so you don''t have to worry about which direction to go. It''s surf-training-wheels, and it works. A two-hour group lesson costs LKR 3,000–4,000 including a soft-top board, rash guard, and basic instruction on how to paddle, pop up, and not get ragdolled. Private lessons run LKR 5,000–6,000. Most schools have you in the water for the first 30 minutes of theory — the instructors are mostly local guys who''ve been surfing Weligama since they were kids. They''ll push you into your first waves and shout timing cues from behind you. By the end of two hours, about 70% of beginners are standing up for at least a few seconds. That first ride, when you feel the board lock into the wave and you''re suddenly gliding, is genuinely addictive. Weligama has dozens of surf schools lining the beach; the best ones are the ones with the smallest class sizes.',
    where_to_experience = 'Weligama Bay is on the south coast, 30 km east of Galle. The surf beach is the eastern end of the bay, past the fishing boat anchorage. Most surf schools are clustered along the main strip, with their boards stacked outside. Look for schools that offer 2-to-1 or 3-to-1 student-to-instructor ratios; the ones with 8-to-1 ratios are just pushing volume. The best beginner waves are in front of the Weligama Bay Resort area, where the reef shelf creates a consistent sand-break. For intermediate surfers, head 2 km east to the Coconut Point break, a left-hander that works on a bigger swell. Further east, Midigama''s Plantations and Lazy Left are for experienced surfers only — the reef is sharp and the takeoff is steep. The main surf season on the south coast runs November to April. Outside these months, the swell is smaller and less consistent, but Weligama still picks up more swell than other west-facing beaches. Lessons are available year-round but November–March is the sweet spot.',
    tips_json = '["Book a lesson early in your trip — you''ll want time to practice on your own after the first lesson.","Rent a board for the rest of the day after your lesson (LKR 500 for 2 hours) to practise what you learned.","The best learning window is 7–9am when the water is glassy and the crowd hasn''t arrived.","Bring reef-safe sunscreen and zinc for your face — the tropical sun bouncing off the water will burn you even on cloudy days.","Don''t buy a new board in Weligama; the rental quiver is fine for learning and you don''t know what you want yet."]',
    best_time = 'November to April is the main season on the south coast. The best learner conditions are December–February when the swell is 2–3 feet and the winds are offshore (smooth waves). Early morning (7–9am) is the best time of day.',
    cta_text = 'Book surf lessons in Weligama'
WHERE slug = 'south-coast-surf-lessons'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 17. sri-lankan-food-market-tour
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'The best way to understand Sri Lankan food is to shop for it where the locals do. A market tour through Pettah, Galle Fort market, or the Kandy central market takes you past piles of rambutan, king coconut, jackfruit, and ten varieties of chilli, followed by a cooking lesson where you learn to turn it all into a proper rice and curry.',
    intro = 'The best way to understand Sri Lankan food is to shop for it where the locals do. A market tour through Pettah, Galle Fort market, or the Kandy central market takes you past piles of rambutan, king coconut, jackfruit, and ten varieties of chilli, followed by a cooking lesson where you learn to turn it all into a proper rice and curry. Most market tours include a guided walk through the produce section, the spice alley, and the fish market (if you''re coastal), followed by a cooking class in a local home. The cost is about LKR 4,000–6,000 per person for a half-day experience, including the ingredients for the class. You''ll learn to make a basic Sri Lankan curry powder from scratch (roasted curry leaves, cumin, coriander, fennel, cinnamon, cardamom, cloves — ground in a mortar), cook dhal with coconut milk (parippu), make a chicken or fish curry using the temper method, and prepare pol sambol (grated coconut with chilli, lime, and Maldive fish). You get to eat everything at the end, which is the best part.',
    where_to_experience = 'For Colombo market tours, the Pettah Market area covers several blocks of chaotic, colourful commerce. Start at the main Pettah bus stand and follow your guide through the spice shops on 3rd Cross Street, the fruit and vegetable section on Prince Street, and the fish market on the canal. Colombo Food Tours runs a good 3-hour walk for LKR 5,000 per person. In Galle, the Galle Fort market area (known as the Dutch Market by locals) is smaller and more manageable, with a great spice shop at the corner of Hospital Street. In Kandy, the central market on Market Street is a two-storey building with produce downstairs and spice merchants upstairs. For cooking classes, the best ones are held in family homes rather than commercial kitchens. The Sri Lankan Cooking Academy in Galle and Tikiri''s Cooking Class in Kandy both get excellent reviews. Inland, Jaffna has a distinctive Northern cuisine — try the crab curry and palmyrah-based desserts. If you''re near the coast, the Negombo fish market at 5am is a spectacle — the night''s catch comes in while auctioneers shout prices in Sinhala and Tamil.',
    tips_json = '["Go to the market with an empty stomach — every stall will offer you samples, from fresh pineapple to fried chickpeas to jaggery chunks.","The best spice shops are on Pettah''s 3rd Cross Street; buy cinnamon quills (true Ceylon cinnamon, not cassia) for about LKR 300 for 100g.","For a cooking class, look for ones that make curry powder from scratch, not from pre-ground mixes.","Bring a reusable bag; the market vendors load you up with produce and single-use plastic is everywhere.","The fish market tour is best at sunrise (5:30–6am) — by 8am the best stuff is sold and the flies have taken over."]',
    best_time = 'Mornings are best for any market tour (6am–9am is prime time). November to April is the most comfortable weather for walking markets. Avoid the monsoon months for the fish market experience — the catch is smaller and the rain makes the streets sloppy.',
    cta_text = 'Book a market and cooking tour'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lankan-food-market-tour'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 18. ayurveda-wellness-retreat
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'An Ayurveda wellness retreat in Sri Lanka is a full lifestyle reset. You get a consultation with a qualified doctor, a customised daily treatment plan, a strict Ayurvedic diet, and accommodation in a setting designed for healing — usually right on the beach or deep in the tea country. It''s not a spa holiday; it''s medicine.',
    intro = 'An Ayurveda wellness retreat in Sri Lanka is a full lifestyle reset. You get a consultation with a qualified doctor, a customised daily treatment plan, a strict Ayurvedic diet, and accommodation in a setting designed for healing — usually right on the beach or deep in the tea country. It''s not a spa holiday; it''s medicine. The minimum stay for any meaningful retreat is 5–7 days, and the effects are cumulative. A 7-day retreat with full board, treatments, and accommodation ranges from LKR 60,000 at a local centre to LKR 200,000+ at a luxury resort. The daily routine starts early with herbal tea (6:30am), followed by yoga, treatment sessions, a strict diet of kitchari and steamed vegetables, afternoon rest, and evening meditation. The treatments typically include Abhyanga (full-body oil massage with herbal oils chosen for your dosha), Shirodhara (warm oil poured in a steady stream on your forehead — deeply calming), and Swedana (herbal steam bath). Most retreats prohibit alcohol, caffeine, and meat during the programme — the idea is to let your digestive system rest completely.',
    where_to_experience = 'The best Ayurveda retreats fall into three categories. Beach retreats: The nine centres along the Beruwala–Bentota coast (the Ayurveda Coast) are the most established. Barberyn Beach Ayurveda Resort in Beruwala has been running 40+ years and operates with on-site doctors who hold morning consultations. Siddhalepa Ayurveda Resort in Wadduwa is more affordable and equally authentic. Hill-country retreats: The cool climate of the central highlands is ideal for detox programmes. The Ayurveda Resort Mandira in Nuwara Eliya combines retreats with tea plantation walks. Purpose-built centres: The Sanctuary Retreat in Galle is a boutique option with 12 rooms and personalised programmes. Lotus Ayurveda in Hikkaduwa offers affordable 7-day packages (LKR 45,000–55,000) without sacrificing quality. Government-registered Ayurveda centres display a badge from the Sri Lanka Ayurveda Medical Council — look for it. Avoid places that call themselves Ayurveda but offer only Swedish massage and a herbal tea.',
    tips_json = '["Book a minimum of 7 days — the first 2–3 days are detox adjustment (headaches, fatigue, cravings are normal) and the real benefits start around day 4.","Tell the doctor every symptom you have, even unrelated ones — Ayurveda treats the whole system, not just the complaint you came with.","The diet is bland for a reason; don''t sneak outside for coffee or fried food — you''ll undo the treatment and make yourself feel worse.","Pack loose cotton clothes; tight synthetics interfere with the oil treatments and steam baths.","Avoid going in with a packed schedule — the retreat routine is full, and the afternoon rest is actually part of the treatment, not free time."]',
    best_time = 'November to April is the traditional Ayurveda season in Sri Lanka. The dry, warm weather allows the body''s channels to open properly during treatments. For hill-country retreats, January–March offers the clearest weather and coolest temperatures (ideal for Panchakarma).',
    cta_text = 'Book your wellness retreat'
WHERE slug = 'ayurveda-wellness-retreat'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 19. buddhist-heritage-pilgrimage
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka has been a centre of Theravada Buddhism for over 2,300 years, and the island is dotted with stupas, temples, monasteries, and pilgrimage sites that draw devotees from across Asia. The main pilgrimage route — from the Temple of the Tooth to Anuradhapura to Mihintale — has been walked by Buddhists for centuries.',
    intro = 'Sri Lanka has been a centre of Theravada Buddhism for over 2,300 years, and the island is dotted with stupas, temples, monasteries, and pilgrimage sites that draw devotees from across Asia. The main pilgrimage route — from the Temple of the Tooth to Anuradhapura to Mihintale — has been walked by Buddhists for centuries. The Temple of the Tooth in Kandy holds one of Buddhism''s most sacred relics: a tooth of the Buddha, brought to Sri Lanka in the 4th century CE hidden in the hair of a princess. The temple''s daily rituals (puja at 5:30am, 10am, and 6:30pm) are open to visitors and well worth attending — the drumming, chanting, and scent of jasmine and sandalwood create an atmosphere that''s genuinely moving. Anuradhapura''s Sri Maha Bodhi is a sapling from the original Bodhi Tree in Bodh Gaya under which the Buddha attained enlightenment, planted in 288 BCE and protected by generations of monks and kings. Mihintale, 12 km east of Anuradhapura, is where Buddhism was first introduced to Sri Lanka — the 1,840 steps to the summit are climbed by pilgrims during the June Poson festival.',
    where_to_experience = 'The three holiest sites are in the Cultural Triangle, all within 100 km of each other. Kandy''s Temple of the Tooth (Sri Dalada Maligawa) is in the centre of Kandy by the lake. The dress code is strict: covered shoulders and knees, shoes off before entering. The relic chamber itself is opened briefly during each puja. Anuradhapura''s sacred city stretches for 40 sq km; the Sri Maha Bodhi is the most revered spot (women should cover their heads within the shrine railings). The Ruwanwelisaya dagoba and Jetavanaramaya are the other major pilgrimage destinations within the city. Mihintale is a hilltop complex reached by climbing the stone staircase; the summit has a monument marking the meeting of Mahinda and King Devanampiya Tissa in 247 BCE. Beyond the main three, other important sites: the Dambulla Cave Temple (golden cave temple with 153 Buddha statues), the Aluvihara Rock Cave Temple in Matale (where the Pali Canon was first written down), and the Somawathiya Chaitya in Polonnaruwa (a quieter pilgrimage spot with less tourist traffic). The Kelaniya Temple near Colombo is a major pilgrimage site for Buddhists from the southern province.',
    tips_json = '["Attend a puja ceremony at the Temple of the Tooth (6:30pm is the most elaborate) — the drumming and chanting are powerful even if you''re not Buddhist.","Climb Mihintale early (6am) to beat the heat and the pilgrims; the 1,840 steps take about 40 minutes.","At the Sri Maha Bodhi, sit quietly for 10 minutes — watching the pilgrims pray is as meaningful as the tree itself.","Carry a white cloth to cover your head at the Bo Tree; women in particular should have a shawl or scarf.","The full-moon (Poya) days are the most special times to visit but also the busiest — temples overflow with pilgrims in white."]',
    best_time = 'December to May is dry and comfortable in the Cultural Triangle. January''s full-moon day (Duruthu Poya) is a major pilgrimage event. Poson Poya in June marks the arrival of Buddhism in Sri Lanka and Mihintale is especially crowded but atmospheric.',
    cta_text = 'Walk the Buddhist pilgrimage route'
WHERE slug = 'buddhist-heritage-pilgrimage'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 20. honeymoon-beach-boutique-stays
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka does boutique better than most. Instead of generic resort chains, you get small hotels carved into cliff faces, colonial villas with private pools in the middle of tea country, and beachfront cabins with four-poster beds and outdoor showers where you can hear the waves from your pillow.',
    intro = 'Sri Lanka does boutique better than most. Instead of generic resort chains, you get small hotels carved into cliff faces, colonial villas with private pools in the middle of tea country, and beachfront cabins with four-poster beds and outdoor showers where you can hear the waves from your pillow. The south coast has the highest concentration of boutique properties, with places like Cape Weligama (perched on a headland with a crescent-shaped infinity pool) and Fort Bazaar in Galle Fort (a converted Dutch merchant house with an inner courtyard and an excellent spa). For something more secluded, the villas at Jetwing Vil Uyana near Sigiriya are built on stilts over lotus-filled paddy fields. On the east coast, Anilana Nilaveli has a beautiful stretch of beach with spacious suites that open directly to the sand. Prices for high-end boutique properties range from LKR 25,000 to LKR 80,000 per night depending on season and inclusions.',
    where_to_experience = 'South coast boutique belt: The stretch from Galle to Tangalle is packed with small, design-led properties. In Galle Fort, Amangalla is the grand dame (colonial luxury, prices to match) while Fort Bazaar offers the same vibe at half the price. In Weligama, W15 and the Surf Hotel are smaller and more intimate than the big resorts. Tangalle has some of the most secluded beachfront properties — the Anantara Peace Haven is the luxury option, but smaller places like The Tangalle Bay Hotel offer character without the price tag. Inland: Jetwing Vil Uyana near Sigiriya and Heritance Tea Factory in Nuwara Eliya are the two most photographed luxury properties in the country — both for good reason. On the east coast (May–October), Uga Bay in Pasikuda and Coral Bay in Trincomalee have the best beachfront rooms. Most boutique hotels include breakfast; many also offer in-house Ayurveda treatments and cooking classes.',
    tips_json = '["Book at least 3 months ahead for December–March (peak season) — the best boutique places have 8–15 rooms and sell out fast.","Many boutique hotels offer direct booking discounts of 10–15% if you skip Booking.com; email them directly.","The south coast boutique hotels have a minimum 3-night stay during Christmas and New Year — plan accordingly.","For absolute privacy, choose a property with fewer than 10 rooms; the 20-room boutique places still feel like small resorts.","Ask about airport transfers — most mid-range and high-end boutique places include a car and driver from Colombo for free on a 5+ night stay."]',
    best_time = 'November to April for the south coast and hill country; May to October for the east coast. Avoid the monsoon transition months (April and October) if you want guaranteed sunshine for a short honeymoon.',
    cta_text = 'Find your boutique stay'
WHERE slug = 'honeymoon-beach-boutique-stays'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 21. luxury-slow-travel-sri-lanka
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Slow travel in Sri Lanka means private drivers, hand-picked villas, and itineraries designed around serendipity rather than ticking boxes. It''s a private tea tasting at a plantation that''s been in the same family for generations, a sunset sail in Galle harbour on a traditional wooden boat, and a private wildlife talk by a former park ranger.',
    intro = 'Slow travel in Sri Lanka means private drivers, hand-picked villas, and itineraries designed around serendipity rather than ticking boxes. It''s a private tea tasting at a plantation that''s been in the same family for generations, a sunset sail in Galle harbour on a traditional wooden boat, and a private wildlife talk by a former park ranger. For most travellers, a slow itinerary covers 3–4 locations over 10–14 days, staying at least 3 nights in each place. The classic slow route: Colombo (1 night), Galle Fort (3–4 nights), the hill country around Nuwara Eliya or Haputale (3–4 nights), and Sigiriya or the Cultural Triangle (3–4 nights). Each move is a private car transfer with a driver who stays with you for the whole trip — costs about $50–70 per day including the car, fuel, and driver''s accommodation. Slow travel costs more upfront but you save on impulse expenses, guides, and organised tours because you have the time to find things yourself.',
    where_to_experience = 'Colombo: Stay at the Colombo Court Hotel or the Kingsbury for city access; request a driver for a morning walk through Pettah market before the crowds. Galle Fort: Spend 3–4 days inside the fort walls — walk the ramparts at different times of day, eat at different cafes, take a cooking class, book a sunrise fishing trip. The Galle Fort Hotel is the classic base; the Fort Bazaar is a smarter, more contemporary alternative. Hill country: Base yourself at the Heritance Tea Factory or a private bungalow on a working tea estate. Ask for a private tour of the factory followed by a tasting of single-estate teas that never leave the estate — the factory manager will usually oblige for a small tip. Cultural Triangle: Jetwing Vil Uyana is the best base for Sigiriya and the ruins. Request a guide for a private, early-morning (5:30am) visit to Sigiriya before the gates open — guides can arrange special early entry with advance notice.',
    tips_json = '["A private driver for the whole trip costs $50–70/day including their accommodation — this isn''t much more than renting a car and navigating yourself, and infinitely better.","Request your guide for Sigiriya by name through a hotel you trust; the guides who stand at the gate vary wildly in quality.","Book activities with flexibility: a sunset sail, a private cooking class, and a guided temple visit at dawn; leave afternoons free.","The best luxury experiences in Sri Lanka are free — walking Galle Fort at 6am, watching mist clear over a tea plantation, sunset at Pidurangala.","Ask your hotel to arrange a home visit (dinner at a local family''s house) — it''s more memorable than any restaurant meal."]',
    best_time = 'November to April for southern coastal and hill-country slow travel. May to October for east coast and Cultural Triangle. The shoulder months (October and April) offer the best of both worlds with fewer crowds.',
    cta_text = 'Design your slow travel itinerary'
WHERE slug = 'luxury-slow-travel-sri-lanka'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 22. sinharaja-rainforest
UPDATE experiences
SET excerpt = 'Sinharaja is Sri Lanka''s last viable area of primary tropical rainforest, a UNESCO World Heritage site that''s been evolving undisturbed for millions of years. The density of endemic species here is staggering — 95% of the canopy birds, 80% of the amphibians, and 50% of the trees and mammals found in the reserve are found nowhere else on earth.',
    intro = 'Sinharaja is Sri Lanka''s last viable area of primary tropical rainforest, a UNESCO World Heritage site that''s been evolving undisturbed for millions of years. The density of endemic species here is staggering — 95% of the canopy birds, 80% of the amphibians, and 50% of the trees and mammals found in the reserve are found nowhere else on earth. The forest covers 111 sq km in the southwestern lowlands, with an elevation that runs from 300 to 1,170 metres, creating multiple layers of habitat. Walking the forest trails means being surrounded by giant dipterocarp trees, lianas the thickness of your arm, and a constant background noise of bird calls, insect hum, and the drip of water from the canopy. A standard guided walk costs LKR 1,500 per person for a 3-hour trek plus the park entry fee (LKR 2,500 for adults). The guides are mandatory — and necessary, because the trails are unmarked and easy to lose. You''ll see endemic birds like the Sri Lankan blue magpie, red-faced malkoha, and green-billed coucal; with luck, you might spot a purple-faced langur, sambar deer, or even a Sri Lankan leopard, though they''re far shyer here than at Yala.',
    where_to_experience = 'The main entrance to Sinharaja is at Kudawa, about 150 km from Colombo and 2 hours from Galle by road. The road from the highway to the entrance is winding and narrow — a tuk-tuk or 4WD is useful for the last 5 km, though regular cars can manage in the dry season. From the Kudawa entrance, the main trail follows a stream through the forest; the guides know the landmarks and the names of the trees. The ascent to the peak of Hinipitigala (1,170 metres) takes about 3 hours from the base and rewards you with views over the canopy. If you''re not up for the climb, the lower trails near the entrance are rich in birdlife and easier walking. The forest is also accessible from the north side (Pitadeniya entrance), which is less frequented and offers a different set of trails. The nearby village of Kudawa has basic guesthouses and homestays (LKR 2,000–4,000 per night) — they''re simple but the forest sounds at night are part of the experience. The best guides are from the Sinharaja Guide Association — based in Kudawa, they know the forest intimately.',
    tips_json = '["Hire a guide from the Sinharaja Guide Association at the Kudawa entrance — they know the bird calls and will spot creatures you''d walk right past.","Leeches are a fact of life here. Wear leech socks (sold at the entrance for LKR 300) or long trousers tucked into your socks.","Start before 7am when the bird activity peaks and the temperature is still cool — by 10am the forest floor is humid and the birds have gone quiet.","The trails get slippery fast; hiking shoes with good grip are non-negotiable.","Bring a rain jacket even in the dry season — Sinharaja is a rainforest and it can rain without warning year-round."]',
    best_time = 'January to April is the least rainy period, though it still rains regularly in a rainforest. August–September is the driest time overall. Avoid May–July and October–November when the monsoon rains make trails treacherous and leech activity hits its peak.',
    cta_text = 'Trek Sinharaja Rainforest'
WHERE slug = 'sinharaja-rainforest'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

